{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01UWVnx9h2R1Va9GdmTDcFpp","type":"tool_result","content":"88\t}\n89\t\n90\t/// To translate between month (int) to month (char).\n91\t///\n92\t/// ```c\n93\t/// static const char *(s_month[]) = {\"Jan\", \"Feb\", ... \"Dec\"};\n94\t/// ```\n95\t/// The C array is `const char *[12]`, i.e. twelve *thin* pointers.  A\n96\t/// `[&CStr; 12]` would be twelve *fat* pointers (ptr + len) and therefore have\n97\t/// the wrong stride, which matters because `copy_mon` reproduces the original's\n98\t/// unchecked indexing below.\n99\t#[repr(transparent)]\n100\tstruct MonthTable([*const c_char; 12]);\n101\t\n102\t// The table is immutable data; the raw pointers only ever point at `'static`\n103\t// string literals.\n104\tunsafe impl Sync for MonthTable {}\n105\t\n106\tstatic S_MONTH: MonthTable = MonthTable([\n107\t    c\"Jan\".as_ptr(),\n108\t    c\"Feb\".as_ptr(),\n109\t    c\"Mar\".as_ptr(),\n110\t    c\"Apr\".as_ptr(),\n111\t    c\"May\".as_ptr(),\n112\t    c\"Jun\".as_ptr(),\n113\t    c\"Jul\".as_ptr(),\n114\t    c\"Aug\".as_ptr(),\n115\t    c\"Sep\".as_ptr(),\n116\t    c\"Oct\".as_ptr(),\n117\t    c\"Nov\".as_ptr(),\n118\t    c\"Dec\".as_ptr(),\n119\t]);\n120\t\n121\t/// `strncpy(fileq->mon, s_month[p->tm_mon], 3)`\n122\t///\n123\t/// The C performs this indexing completely unchecked, so a `tm_mon` outside\n124\t/// `0..=11` reads past the end (or before the start) of `s_month` and hands\n125\t/// `strncpy` whatever pointer-sized value happens to live there.  On the\n126\t/// reference build that faults for `tm_mon` in `-3..=-1` and `>= 12`, and copies\n127\t/// junk for a few more-negative values.  This is undefined behaviour in the\n128\t/// original, and the translation reproduces it verbatim rather than adding a\n129\t/// range check the C does not have — inserting one would make the Rust *return\n130\t/// normally* for inputs on which the C traps.\n131\t#[inline]\n132\tunsafe fn copy_mon(fileq: *mut file_queue, tm_mon: c_int) {\n133\t    let entry = *S_MONTH.0.as_ptr().offset(tm_mon as isize);\n134\t    strncpy((*fileq).mon.as_mut_ptr(), entry, 3);\n135\t}\n136\t\n137\t/// ```c"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1ae76093-19c1-48bd-ad47-ef026a38794e","uuid":"37d770d8-23a2-48c3-b2c1-7c21aa5afc1f","timestamp":"2026-08-20T09:01:41.599Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-I0kTQU/translated_rust/src/file_queue.rs","content":"}\n\n/// To translate between month (int) to month (char).\n///\n/// ```c\n/// static const char *(s_month[]) = {\"Jan\", \"Feb\", ... \"Dec\"};\n/// ```\n/// The C array is `const char *[12]`, i.e. twelve *thin* pointers.  A\n/// `[&CStr; 12]` would be twelve *fat* pointers (ptr + len) and therefore have\n/// the wrong stride, which matters because `copy_mon` reproduces the original's\n/// unchecked indexing below.\n#[repr(transparent)]\nstruct MonthTable([*const c_char; 12]);\n\n// The table is immutable data; the raw pointers only ever point at `'static`\n// string literals.\nunsafe impl Sync for MonthTable {}\n\nstatic S_MONTH: MonthTable = MonthTable([\n    c\"Jan\".as_ptr(),\n    c\"Feb\".as_ptr(),\n    c\"Mar\".as_ptr(),\n    c\"Apr\".as_ptr(),\n    c\"May\".as_ptr(),\n    c\"Jun\".as_ptr(),\n    c\"Jul\".as_ptr(),\n    c\"Aug\".as_ptr(),\n    c\"Sep\".as_ptr(),\n    c\"Oct\".as_ptr(),\n    c\"Nov\".as_ptr(),\n    c\"Dec\".as_ptr(),\n]);\n\n/// `strncpy(fileq->mon, s_month[p->tm_mon], 3)`\n///\n/// The C performs this indexing completely unchecked, so a `tm_mon` outside\n/// `0..=11` reads past the end (or before the start) of `s_month` and hands\n/// `strncpy` whatever pointer-sized value happens to live there.  On the\n/// reference build that faults for `tm_mon` in `-3..=-1` and `>= 12`, and copies\n/// junk for a few more-negative values.  This is undefined behaviour in the\n/// original, and the translation reproduces it verbatim rather than adding a\n/// range check the C does not have — inserting one would make the Rust *return\n/// normally* for inputs on which the C traps.\n#[inline]\nunsafe fn copy_mon(fileq: *mut file_queue, tm_mon: c_int) {\n    let entry = *S_MONTH.0.as_ptr().offset(tm_mon as isize);\n    strncpy((*fileq).mon.as_mut_ptr(), entry, 3);\n}\n\n/// ```c","numLines":50,"startLine":88,"totalLines":354}}}
